\frametitle{14. Proposition 3: paper $\rightarrow $ Lean $\rightarrow $ proof}
\scriptsize
\textbf{Paper statement.} If $0\le p^1_k\le p^2_k\le1$, then
$\Delta C(s)=\sum_{k\in U}[(1-p^1_k)^{s+1}-(1-p^2_k)^{s+1}]\ge0$; when
$U\ne\varnothing$ and $p^1_k=0<p^2_k<1$, the gap is strictly increasing and concave.

\textbf{Lean translation and proof fragment.}
\begin{lstlisting}[language={}]
theorem dynamic_cumulative_language_effect :
  dynamic_cumulative_language_effectSpec := by
  intro Language _ unfamiliar p1 p2 hHazards
  dsimp; constructor
  case left =>
    intro s; apply Finset.sum_nonneg; intro k hk
    have h := hHazards k hk
    -- apply power monotonicity to 0 <= 1-p2 <= 1-p1
    ...
  case right =>
    -- closed-frontier branch: geometric powers fall
    ...
\end{lstlisting}
\textbf{Plain English.} A higher first-use hazard lowers every survival
probability $(1-p)^{s+1}$; summing those nonnegative differences proves the
weak result. Strict geometric decay supplies growth and diminishing increments.

